    {
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "cause": "`${TARGET%%-*}` assumes the Rust triple's arch component is spelled the same way xwin spells it. That holds for x86_64 and aarch64 only; Rust's `i686`/`i586` map to xwin `x86` and `thumbv7a` maps to xwin `aarch`.",
      "checklist_ids": [
        4,
        8
      ],
      "confidence": "medium",
      "description": "The xwin branch accepts any `*-pc-windows-msvc` target and then derives `XWIN_ARCH` from the triple prefix (`${TARGET%%-*}`). cargo-xwin/xwin's `--xwin-arch` accepts only `x86`, `x86_64`, `aarch`, `aarch64`. For the two other MSVC triples a caller can legitimately put in the `targets` override \u2014 `i686-pc-windows-msvc` (prefix `i686`) and `thumbv7a-pc-windows-msvc` (prefix `thumbv7a`) \u2014 the exported value is not an xwin arch name, so `cargo xwin build` aborts on an invalid `XWIN_ARCH` value on a leg that would have built fine had the variable been left unset (xwin would then pick the arch itself). P2 because this is incorrect behaviour on a reachable input: `docs/reusable-workflows.md:884` explicitly invites callers to override the matrix with their own Windows target, and `docs/workflow-contract.md:1041` documents `XWIN_ARCH` as \"derived from the target triple\" without noting that only x86_64/aarch64 derive correctly.",
      "evidence_style": "speculative",
      "file": "scripts/ci/release/build-rust-binary.sh",
      "fingerprint": "bb2319845575cdc2",
      "fix": "Map the triple prefix to xwin's arch vocabulary instead of passing it through, and reject (or leave `XWIN_ARCH` unset for) prefixes with no mapping so the build keeps xwin's own default rather than an invalid value.",
      "line": 56,
      "ordinal": 1,
      "severity": "P2",
      "since_round": 1,
      "status": "open",
      "title": "Derived XWIN_ARCH is invalid for i686/thumbv7a MSVC targets"
    },
    {
      "category": "test-gap",
      "cause": "`grep -v '^\\s*#'` relies on a GNU-only escape; the test's own comment states the intent (\"Comments may name it; expressions may not\"), which silently does not happen under BSD grep, and the workflow does carry `github.job` inside a comment.",
      "checklist_ids": [
        14,
        24
      ],
      "confidence": "medium",
      "description": "The guard that `github.job` never appears in an expression strips comment lines with `grep -v '^\\s*#'`. `\\s` is a GNU extension; BSD grep (macOS, the stated dev platform here) treats `\\s` as the literal character `s`, so the pattern becomes `^s*#` and never matches the 4-space-indented comments in the workflow. reusable-test-rust-build.yml:211 contains the comment `# never github.job (null outside execution steps).`, so on macOS the second grep matches that comment, exits 0, and `assert_failure` fails \u2014 the test reports a violation that does not exist. P3 because the production workflow is correct and the defect is confined to test portability (it still passes on the GNU-grep CI runner); only the local-run reliability of this new test is affected.",
      "evidence_style": "cross_file",
      "file": "tests/bats/integration/test_reusable_test_rust_build.bats",
      "fingerprint": "b6408bcdd770c50b",
      "fix": "Use the POSIX character class: `grep -vE '^[[:space:]]*#'`.",
      "line": 44,
      "ordinal": 1,
      "severity": "P3",
      "since_round": 1,
      "status": "open",
      "title": "Comment-stripping grep uses non-portable \\s class"
    },
    {
      "category": "test-gap",
      "cause": "A line-oriented grep is used to validate a YAML structure whose values legally span multiple lines; it only sees inline scalars.",
      "checklist_ids": [
        18,
